      "text": "To determine which segment is the longest, we need to analyze the angles and the lengths of the sides that are involved.\n\n1. **Identify the angles shown in the figure**:\n   - ∠CAD = 55°\n   - ∠CBD = 40°\n   - ∠CAB = 75° (alternate interior angle)\n\n2. **Use the Law of Sines** to find the lengths of the sides:\n   - Let \\( AC = a \\), \\( BC = b \\), and \\( BD = c \\).\n   - We know the angles opposite the sides, so we can use the Law of Sines to find the lengths.\n\n3. **Calculate the angles formed at point B**:\n   - ∠ABD = ∠CBD + ∠CAB = 40° + 75° = 115°\n   - ∠DBC = 180° - (∠CBD + ∠CAB) = 180° - 115° = 65°\n   - ∠ABD is supplementary to ∠DBC, so ∠DBC = 65°. Therefore, ∠DBA = 65°.\n\n4. **Determine the lengths using the Law of Sines**:\n   - \\( \\frac{a}{\\sin(115°)} = \\frac{b}{\\sin(65°)} = \\frac{c}{\\sin(40°)} \\)\n\n5. **Calculate the lengths**:\n   - For segment \\( AD \\):\n     \\[\n     AD = AC = a\n     \\]\n   - For segment \\( CD \\):\n     \\[\n     CD = AC = a\n     \\]\n   - For segment \\( BD \\):\n     \\[\n     BD = b = \\frac{b \\cdot \\sin(40°)}{\\sin(65°)}\n     \\]\n\n6. **Compare the segments**:\n   - We know that \\( \\sin(65°) > \\sin(40°) \\).\n   - Therefore, \\( b > a \\).\n   - Hence, the longest segment is \\( BD \\).\n\nThe final answer is \\( \\boxed{BD} \\).",
